---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TrabecularBoneViewer from '../../components/TrabecularBoneViewer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Trabecular Bone Viewer"
  description="A browser based viewer for progressively refined trabecular bone surfaces."
>
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="kicker">Research tool · Prototype</p>
    <h1>Inspect trabecular bone in the browser.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      This prototype is designed for fast first visualisation. It opens a small overview surface first,
      then replaces it with finer geometry only when more detail is useful.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="shell viewer-intro-grid">
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Local first</p>
        <h2>Your specimen can stay on your computer.</h2>
        <p>
          Select a prepared specimen folder below. The files are read by the browser from your device.
          Local mode does not upload them to this website.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Progressive detail</p>
        <h2>Do not wait for native detail before moving the model.</h2>
        <p>
          The viewer starts with an overview mesh, can refine automatically through medium detail,
          and loads the native surface only when you explicitly request it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section viewer-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <TrabecularBoneViewer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">How the prototype works</p>
          <h2>One segmentation, several surfaces.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          The preparation step converts a binary volume into several gzip compressed GLB meshes.
          Coarse levels are for navigation. The final level is extracted from the native segmentation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="viewer-pipeline">
        <li>
          <span>01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Prepare once</h3>
            <p>Convert an OME TIFF or TIFF segmentation into a viewer folder with a manifest and surface levels.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Open the overview</h3>
            <p>The smallest surface appears first so rotation, zoom, and pan become available quickly.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Refine only when useful</h3>
            <p>Preview and medium surfaces replace the overview without resetting the camera.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Request native detail</h3>
            <p>The largest mesh is never required simply to begin looking at the specimen.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <aside class="viewer-science-note">
        <strong>Scientific caution.</strong>
        <p>
          The overview, preview, and medium meshes are visual navigation approximations. In the current
          converter they use max pooling before surface extraction, which can alter thin structures and topology.
          Use the native detail level for structural inspection when those differences matter.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell compact-callout">
      <p class="kicker">Next engineering step</p>
      <h2>Replace whole specimen levels with spatial chunks.</h2>
      <p>
        This prototype tests the interaction model and gives us real measurements for file size, decompression,
        parsing, first render time, and frame rate. If the native surface becomes too large, the next version
        will divide it spatially and refine only the visible regions.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button secondary" href="/research/topology-bone-fragility/">Back to the bone research interface</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .viewer-intro-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .viewer-intro-grid article {
      padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.34);
    }

    .viewer-intro-grid h2 {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
      line-height: 1.1;
    }

    .viewer-intro-grid p:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .viewer-section {
      padding-top: 2.5rem;
    }

    .viewer-pipeline {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0,1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
    }

    .viewer-pipeline li {
      padding: 1.2rem;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--pine);
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.3);
    }

    .viewer-pipeline > li > span {
      color: var(--terracotta);
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
    }

    .viewer-pipeline h3 {
      margin: .55rem 0 .35rem;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }

    .viewer-pipeline p {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .viewer-science-note {
      margin-top: 2rem;
      padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
      border: 1px solid rgba(197,164,93,.55);
      border-radius: 14px;
      background: rgba(197,164,93,.08);
    }

    .viewer-science-note p {
      margin: .3rem 0 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    @media (max-width: 900px) {
      .viewer-pipeline {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr));
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 680px) {
      .viewer-intro-grid,
      .viewer-pipeline {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
